  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how joint or limb biology may be shaped across life by epigenetic regulation of gene activity. On the left, it organizes influences by developmental stage: in the embryo, factors such as fetus movement and embryonic pathways; in childhood, mechanical loading and biomechanical changes; and in adulthood, trauma, infection, and inflammation. Small human silhouettes highlight joints or limb regions affected at different ages.\n\nThese life-stage inputs are linked to a central cell/nucleus schematic, then to DNA wrapped around histones, suggesting chromatin-level regulation. The right side emphasizes DNA methylation, shown as “Me” groups attached along the DNA strand, and connects this to gene transcription. A highlighted genomic region appears to control expression of position-specific developmental genes. Examples shown include different HOX genes associated with different anatomical sites, such as hand, shoulder, and knee.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that mechanical, developmental, and inflammatory experiences over the lifespan can influence the epigenetic state of cells, especially DNA methylation, which in turn affects transcriptional programs and may help preserve or alter anatomical identity in tissues.",
